<!--
    GH-182: indexed stack arrays get no real bounds.

    Witness: betaflight_STM32F405 (gcc -O2, stripped Cortex-M4 firmware),
    cliServo @ 0x801b4a4 (the firmware is stripped; the names here are the
    test's own, read off the p-code).  The source declares
    `int16_t arguments[6]` at sp+28 and fills it with ONE indexed store,
    `strh.w r0,[r6,#-12]` with r6 = sp + 40 + 2*i, i in [0,5] (the loop guard
    is `cmp r6,#5; bgt error`).  Elements 0..3 are then read at [sp+28..34]
    and elements 4 and 5 at [sp+36]/[sp+38] - all twelve bytes are one object.

    The real array extent can only come from the STORE guard: heritage records
    a LoadGuard for the indexed store, `option loadguardrange` (default on)
    runs the ValueSet solver over the guard's pointer
    (Heritage::analyzeNewLoadGuards, heritage.cc:834) and locks the guard to
    [sp+28, sp+38] step 2, and MapState::addGuard (varmap.cc:1033) turns that
    into an open RangeHint with highind = ((max-min)+1)/step - 1 = 5.  The
    open range then fills to the next mapped local, so the array prints with
    8 elements (same as stock Ghidra's `short local_24 [8]`) and every access
    goes through it in bounds.

    With the option off the guard keeps the whole-space range with step 0,
    addGuard bails, and the only bound is the min_items = 3 fallback:
    RangeHint::attempt_join rejects the stack-0x1c hint on diffsz(4) >
    highind(3), so the array caps at `int2 v7 [4]` written at v7[v3] with v3
    provably reaching 5 (an out-of-bounds subscript on the printed
    declaration), and elements 4 and 5 split off as `int2 v8`/`int2 v9` -
    stack scalars that are range-checked and stored into the servo config but
    never assigned anywhere in the function.

    Pass 1 (default) asserts the fix: one array, all subscripts in bounds,
    elements 4 and 5 read as v7[4]/v7[5], no split-off scalars.  Pass 2
    (`option loadguardrange off`) pins the buggy split shape.  Assertion
    counts span both passes.  The seven callees are stubbed `bx lr` at their
    real addresses.
-->
